WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:04.970
When Stranger Things debuted on Netflix in
2016, it brought a brand-new generation of

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:07.140
bright young stars into the Hollywood spotlight.

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:11.059
The acclaimed sci-fi series continued this
pattern with its second season by tacking

00:00:11.059 --> 00:00:15.780
new characters Max Mayfield and Billy Hargrove
onto the Stranger Things roster and giving

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:18.960
the actors who play them a shot at Netflix-backed
super-stardom.

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:23.320
The man behind the callous-yet-cool bad boy
Billy caught many fans' eyes on seasons 2

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:23.820
and 3.

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:28.580
And as Billy's personal arc became more complex,
his performance grew increasingly captivating.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.580
"Looks like you got some fire in you after
all, huh?

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:39.300
I've been wanting to meet this King Steve
everybody's been telling me so much about."

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:43.020
Viewers couldn't help but feel that his striking
looks were oddly familiar.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:45.060
So, who is the man behind Billy Hargrove?

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:49.600
He's Dacre Montgomery, an Australian actor
and poet who had only a handful of credits

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:52.780
under his belt before securing the role of
Billy on Stranger Things.

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:56.820
Though his filmography is short, there are
a few projects Montgomery has been in that

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:57.780
fans will recognize.

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:02.270
Born in Perth, Western Australia to Canadian
mother Judith Barrett-Lennard and New Zealander

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:06.360
father Scott Montgomery-Harvey, Montgomery
began pursuing acting when he was still a

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:10.640
child an interest partly fostered by his parents,
who were both in the film industry.

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:12.560
"My mom worked in the film industry for 20
years.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:13.500
She was a production coordinator.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:17.280
My dad's still a sound recordist, and um,
I'm lucky that I had that background to fall

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:17.780
in love with it."

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:22.619
He explained a little more about that background
in a March 2017 interview with Teen Vogue,

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:23.120
saying:

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:26.720
"They both worked behind the camera, so I
like to think that I have a really good understanding

00:01:26.729 --> 00:01:30.000
of how all the parts of the puzzle come together
to make a film or TV show.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.580
I always wanted to get into acting.

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:33.600
I've been pursuing that for a long time."

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:37.600
Montgomery continued to explore his passion
in his adolescence, then honed his talents

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:41.079
throughout his adulthood by completing an
acting degree through the Western Australian

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:43.760
Academy of Performing Arts at Edith Cowan
University.

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:48.500
This led Montgomery to roles in short films
like "Betrand the Terrible" and "Godot's Clinic,"

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:52.960
as well as the Australian flick A Few Less
Men, a follow-up to the comedy from Down Under

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:54.260
entitled A Few Best Men.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:59.170
Those experiences then set him on the road
to a big break, which came with the 2017 Power

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:00.120
Rangers reboot.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:03.660
Montgomery appeared as Jason Scott, better
known as the Red Ranger.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:06.880
"It's morphin' time"

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:14.720
Audiences were so impressed with the new kid
on the block that they came together to vote

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:17.180
for him at the 2017 Teen Choice Awards.

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:21.860
For his performance as the Red Ranger, Montgomery
scored a nomination for Choice Sci-Fi Movie

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:25.780
Actor, but sadly lost out to Guardians of
the Galaxy Vol. 2 star Chris Pratt.

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:29.960
After taking a turn as the mighty morphin'
Power Ranger, Montgomery appeared as Jeremy

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:34.110
in the Christmas-themed horror pic Better
Watch Out, described by critics as, quote,

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:38.400
"an ingenious and efficient black-comic shocker"
bolstered by its charismatic cast.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:42.320
Now, if Montgomery's screen work isn't the
reason why he looks so familiar to you, you

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:46.280
might be a music fiend with an affinity for
Australian music and a fantastic memory.

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:51.100
In 2015, Montgomery appeared in the music
video for "Old Souls" by Perth heavy metal

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:52.240
band Make Them Suffer.

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:57.090
He circled back to the music scene in 2017
for indie-folk group Angus &amp; Julia Stone's

00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:57.940
"Chateau" video.

00:02:58.270 --> 00:03:02.440
Of course, Montgomery is best known as Billy
on Stranger Things, and Season 3 saw him display

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:06.271
a much wider range of talent that's sure to
keep him in Hollywood's eye for a long time

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:06.780
to come.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:11.820
Next up for the actor is Justin Kurzel's film
True History of the Kelly Gang, so Montgomery's

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:14.780
mug will remain on screens everywhere for
the foreseeable future.

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:20.200
Staunch Montgomery fans can also tune into
the actor's podcast "DKMH," for which he collaborated

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:22.340
with musicians to bring his poetry to life.

